<aside><title>The discrete logistic equation</title>
<p>Let <m>P_t</m> denote the population size in time period <m>t</m>.  Let <m>M</m> be the carrying capacity, which is the population size that the environment can support.  Then, </p>
<men name="logistic">P_{t+1} - P_t = r \, P_t \, \left( 1 - \frac{P_t}{M} \right)</men>
<p>Equation <ref to="$logistic" /> is the discrete logistic equation.</p>

<ul><li>
  <m>\displaystyle \frac{P_t}{M}</m> is the fraction of the carrying capacity that is used, and</li>
  <li><m>\displaystyle 1 - \frac{P_t}{M}</m> is the unused fraction of the carrying capacity.</li>
</ul>
</aside>
  
<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />

<p>The growth of a population over time is modeled by a discrete dynamical system. Since it is not realistic to assume that exponential growth of the population will be maintained indefinitely in the presence of environmental constraints, a parameter called the ____________________ is introduced into the equations describing the discrete dynamical system. </p>

<answer name="ans1a">
  <choice>low density growth rate</choice>
  <choice credit="1">carrying capacity</choice>
  <choice>equilibrium point</choice>
</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<figure suppressFigureNameInCaption="true">
  <image source="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Eulemur_mongoz_%28male_-_face%29.jpg/1920px-Eulemur_mongoz_%28male_-_face%29.jpg" size="small" />
  <caption>Lemur population modeled by a discrete logistic equation<footnote>By IParjan - Own work, Public Domain, https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=2867040</footnote></caption>
  <description>A photograph of a brown lemur sitting on a branch</description>
  </figure>
<p>The growth of a certain population of lemurs in Madagascar is modeled by the discrete logistic equation</p>
  <me>P_{t+1} - P_t = r P_t \left(1 - \frac{P_t}{M}\right)</me>
  
where, <m>P_t</m> is the lemur population at time <m>t</m>, <m>r</m> is the low density growth rate of the population, and <m>M</m> is the carrying capacity. If, at a certain time <m>t</m>, measurements of the lemur population indicate that the value of the fraction <m>\frac{P_t}{M} \approx 1</m>, this could mean that
  <answer name="ans2a">
    <choice>the growth of the lemur population is well-approximated by the exponential growth model at time <m>t</m>.</choice>
    <choice>the lemur population will decline until it eventually reaches the carrying capacity, <m>M</m>.</choice>
    <choice credit="1">environmental conditions are such that the lemur population is at an equilibrium, neither growing nor declining in size.</choice>
  </answer>
</exercise>
</problem>
</section>
